turkey has changed a lot since i visited  10 years ago for one the turkish lira is  significantly weaker so this delicious  turkish coffee that cost me roughly 4 us  dollars back in 2012 would have cost me  just 50 cents with today's conversion  rate and secondly when i was in istanbul  i didn't see a single crypto exchange i  mean back then people were just starting  to wrap their heads around what the  blockchain even was but today crypto  exchanges and ads can be seen all over  the city so why am i pointing these  things out because today inflation is  soaring in turkey it's much higher than  it is in other parts of the world  and many turks are piling into risky  cryptocurrencies  all of this is happening largely due to  recent government policies so i'm  heading into the office to learn more  about what's been going on in turkey and  how it could affect the broader global  economy let's go  so before we talk about crypto we have  to talk about inflation and why the  turkish lira fell so quickly see usually  central banks raise interest rates to  contain inflation and many countries  around the world have started to do that  but turkish president recep tayyip  erdogan has called high interest rates  the mother and father of all evil that's  a real quote from 2018.  not only has he demanded interest rate  cuts as part of an unorthodox strategy  to drive growth but he's fired nearly  every senior turkish official who  opposes his vision  so check this out  you'll see that as turkey's central bank  cut its key policy rate in the fall the  value of the turkish lira plunged  now many turks are struggling to buy  food fuel and other essentials protests  have broken out in the streets and some  citizens have called on erdogan to  resign  so far erdogan has shown no signs of  reversing course and he has maintained  that cutting rates will boost growth  and that is where crypto comes  in turks have been piling into  cryptocurrencies to escape the plunging  lira even though many of those  cryptocurrencies are just as volatile  and get this they're investing in crypto  despite the fact that turkey banned  crypto as a form of payment last year  and even though erdogan has signaled  more regulations are coming the turkish  era has been volatile  than bitcoin and the other coins  and  so we can invest in crypto bravely  addition to talking to a few turks i sat  down with my colleague caitlin ostroff  virtually since she's been reporting on  the trend i think there's a little bit  of a feeling that you know crypto is  even though it might have massive price  fluctuations  is here to stay and even when it's  fallen there's always been a level of  support that it's found  caitlin says in past downturns turks  have moved their money into dollars  euros and gold and many are still doing  that today  but the turkish government has tried for  years to get people to move their bank  deposits back to lira and lately it's  been ramping up those efforts so  what some people fear is that if the  government is not successful in that  they may resort to more draconian  measures of getting people out of  dollars and euros that's so interesting  so it's  a lot of this is just driven by distrust  yeah there's there's massive distrust  and that helps explain why many turks  are also turning to stable coins like  tether these are cryptocurrencies whose  value is pegged to traditional  currencies like the dollar as this data  from crypto compare shows us turks  increasingly move their money into  tether as both rates and the value of  the lira fell if this trend continues if  more and more turks move their money  into cryptocurrencies and exit the  turkish financial system altogether what  could that mean for the country  i mean it would be really destabilizing  and would probably lead to capital  controls so that's kind of the thing  that  everyone's been watching and frankly  waiting for to happen in turkey at some  point  so what does all of this mean for the  broader global economy well turkey's  central bank has signaled that it's done  cutting rates for now and the government  announced a rescue plan to encourage  people to hold the lira but analysts  still expect inflation to continue to  rise and the value of the lira to remain  under pressure and some fear that  further turmoil in turkey could have  bigger economic and geopolitical  implications some of the concern is that  if turkey goes into an economic tailspin  who are they going to get funding and  aid from and that's a little bit up in  the air  to the u.s and the west turkey is seen  as a critical economic partner nato  member and geopolitical ally in the  middle east  so if turkey turned to a country like  russia or china for economic aid it  would rattle nations around the world  so finally what does this mean for the  broader crypto community the fact that  turks are moving their money into  cryptocurrencies is unlikely to trigger  big price moves in them but if erdogan  responds by cracking down on crypto it  could potentially weigh on the value of  bitcoin and other digital assets at  least temporarily so i'd love to hear  from you what do you think is the best  way to protect yourself from inflation  and depreciation in other words what  would you do if you were in turkey leave  me a comment below and if you learned  something from this video be sure to  subscribe to the channel for more  stories like this one i'll see you next  time
 

